  "explanation": "Move semantics and rvalue references are advanced C++ features introduced in C++11 that enable efficient transfer of resources from temporary objects (rvalues) to new objects, minimizing unnecessary copying. They are essential for writing high-performance code, especially when managing dynamic resources like memory, file handles, or network connections. By allowing objects to 'move' rather than copy their internal data, C++ developers can optimize resource management and improve program efficiency.\n\nMove semantics in C++ enable efficient transfer of resources from temporary objects (rvalues) to new objects, minimizing unnecessary copying and improving performance. Rvalue references (denoted by &&) are a key feature introduced in C++11 that allow functions to distinguish between lvalues (named objects) and rvalues (temporary objects), facilitating move semantics and perfect forwarding.\n\nMove semantics in C++ enable efficient transfer of resources from temporary objects (rvalues) to new objects, reducing unnecessary copying and improving performance. Rvalue references (denoted by &&) are a key feature introduced in C++11 that allow functions to distinguish between lvalues and rvalues, facilitating move semantics and perfect forwarding in generic code.",
